tion. No failed services, no unnecessary firewall exposure beyond the required postgresql service, and package health checks are clean on all nodes post-executor.","evidence_ids":["cmd-de2e6ff211174be4","cmd-c5428130046c4e56","cmd-a9c275293d3d4aec","cmd-2a187dc4c4fd4b7b","cmd-72f8d5159a5344bd","cmd-3f7716a714344230","cmd-ebd2809838764461","cmd-539143eebdaf4b10","cmd-8f005afc43124045","cmd-61ce269e351e4722","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health","global:after_executor:node3:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:after_executor:node3:failed-services"]},"overall_summary":"The executor established three-node PostgreSQL 16 streaming replication (node1 primary, node2/node3 standbys), directly demonstrated a bounded failover in which node2 was promoted to writable primary with zero committed-data loss, repointed node3 to the new primary, and rebuilt node1 as a standby of node2 — fully satisfying the task's core failover/recovery contract with concrete before/during/after evidence rather than configuration alone. The mandatory provider-wide restart protocol was also completed and cross-checked cleanly against the fixed before-prepare/after-executor boot-identity snapshots for all three nodes, including the primary (node2), each showing changed boot ids and full service/data/cluster recovery.","limitations":["The pg_basebackup setup phase required several failed/retried attempts (proxy timeouts, background-process orphaning, empty-directory errors) before succeeding; while these were self-corrected with no lasting effect, they add noise to the timeline and slightly reduce certainty about the exact mechanism of the final successful basebackup invocation.","Command outputs are captured evidence snippets and may not represent every intermediate state; reliance is placed on the specific psql/pg_stat_replication queries selected by the executor."]},"ttft